snippet.textDisplay:
@heron6462 "to create a full working backup of the National Grid" ummm... why???? Anyway, lets say that is the problem to solve.
The volumetric capacity of LiFePO4 batteries is between 227 to 396 Wh/L - let's take it to 300Wh/L. UK daily electricity consumption is 880  to 900 GWh of electricity.  To store the entire electricity daily need of UK in LiFePO4 batteries, one needs 900e9/300 = 3e9 litres of batteries, or 3e6 cubic metres. Assuming you stack the batteries 3m high (single story house), the area needed to store all the batteries is 1e6 square metres - which is exactly 1 square kilometer.
*Has UK shrank that much?*

