@earnienew OK, but basically, that is just a distruption and a new tool. Now look, you can do all the work of three people. You will make three or even ten times the amount of programs and drivers and such. A business would not look at that and say, "Ah, let's make the identical amount of stuff we used to make and fire 90% of our staff." They will say, "Get the new kid trained on this AI and print me MONEY!"

This is what I think will happen: At first the juniors may be slaughtered. They will lose their jobs... but then the industry will grow, and they will just do it slightly differently. We actually saw this with the computer. A computer used to be a literal job for a guy to do math all day. Then we invented a machine for it. But math didn't go anywhere. We still need math. Now we have firmware programmers.

I think most of the fear about AI replacing jobs is unfounded. It is either not going to be as effective as people market it to be, or we will just adjust and instantly demand ten times the productivity.

